# wpus — ignore rules
#
# Rule of thumb: if a file is generated, machine-specific, private to the
# maintainers, or would tell a reader more than it should, it does not belong
# in the repository. Anything a contributor needs to build the binary or run
# the tests IS tracked (source, tests, fixtures, product docs, CI config).
#
# Every section explains itself: a rule whose purpose is unclear gets deleted
# by the next person, and then the file it was protecting gets committed.

# --- Build output -----------------------------------------------------------
/wpus
/wpus.exe
/dist/
*.test
*.out
*.exe

# --- Test and profiling output ---------------------------------------------
coverage/
coverage.out
coverage.html
coverage.txt
*.prof
*.pprof

# --- Generated scan output --------------------------------------------------
# Produced when dogfooding wpus inside its own repository. Root-anchored so a
# fixture deeper in the tree can never be hidden by these patterns.
/results.sarif
/report.sarif
/scan.json
/report.json
/baseline.json
/wpus-baseline.json

# --- Release archives -------------------------------------------------------
# GoReleaser writes into /dist/ (above); these catch artefacts built by hand
# or unzipped into the repository root.
/*.tar.gz
/*.tgz
/*.zip

# --- Private research and internal documents --------------------------------
# Research dumps live in dated folders (docs/11:09:2026/, docs/2026-09-11/)
# and contain internal audits of this tool: the weaknesses they describe are a
# roadmap for an attacker, so they are maintained locally, never published.
# Both naming conventions are covered; a dated folder cannot be tracked by
# accident.
docs/?[0-9]:[0-9][0-9]:[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]/
docs/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]/
docs/research/
docs/private/
docs/internal/
*-internal.md
*.private.md
*.local.md
# Maintainer scratch notes (a tracked NOTES.md needs an explicit `!NOTES.md`).
NOTES.md

# --- Tooling packs and caches -----------------------------------------------
# repomix (and similar) pack the whole tree into one file: large, redundant,
# and stale the moment it is written.
repomix-output*.xml

# --- Local environment and secrets ------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# Local overrides of a committed policy file (.wpus.yaml itself is a product
# feature — a repository may commit one for CI — so only overrides are hidden).
.wpus.local.yaml
.wpus.local.yml
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore

# --- OS and editor state ----------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
._*
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
.fleet/
.history/
.project
.settings/
.nbproject/
*.swp
*.swo
*.kate-swp
*~
\#*\#
.\#*

# --- Agent and AI-tooling state ---------------------------------------------
# Local agent configuration and transcripts. Documentation written for agents
# (docs/agents.md, AGENTS.md) is intentional and stays tracked; only the
# tools' own state directories are ignored.
.claude/
.cursor/
.opencode/
.continue/
.aider*
.crush/

# --- Go workspace and vendoring ---------------------------------------------
go.work
go.work.sum
# Not vendored by design; remove this line if the project ever vendors on
# purpose (ignore rules cannot be negated back for the contents of a directory).
/vendor/

# --- Temporary files --------------------------------------------------------
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.orig
*.log
# The committed demo fixture is an intentional log file: keep it visible so it
# cannot be lost by a cleanup that trusts the rule above.
!testdata/demo-site/wp-content/debug.log
!.gitkeep

# --- Local runtime state ----------------------------------------------------
# Nothing today (caches live in the per-user cache directory), but keep a
# scan's scratch output from landing in the tree.
/.cache/
/tmp/
/temp/
